00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.600
to hundreds of hours of output, which is what we want.

91
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.160
So now going deeper into the topic,

92
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.360
let's first differentiate what is custom work versus productized packages.

93
00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.560
When you're dealing with custom work, then every project is different,

94
00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:12.280
meaning you scope it from scratch, you code it individually,

95
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:13.560
and you build something unique.

96
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:15.520
And for your first few or so clients,

97
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:17.680
then this is important because you need to understand

98
00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.760
what are the patterns that are emerging so that you can come up with a more

99
00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:21.880
productized offer.

100
00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.160
And a productized offer or package is basically a defined solution

101
00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:26.480
that is given at a set price.

102
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.280
So it's the same deliverable, even if there's different clients,

103
00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.700
but it's a templated approach that would apply regardless of who you are

104
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.440
talking to.

105
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:36.160
So the core difference, custom, you ask, what do you need?

106
00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.080
And for the productized offer, you just say, here's what I offer.

107
00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:43.720
And each productized package is always specific, concrete, and clear.

108
00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.560
And what that means is before, let's say you tell your client

109
00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.420
that you build AI automations for businesses and you can just chat about what

110
00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:50.920
they need.

111
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.760
If it's a productized offer, you actually give the package itself a name.

112
00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:58.720
To give an example, maybe you name this as an AI lead follow-up system.

113
00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:02.040
So if your name is Nexus Labs, the Nexus Labs lead follow-up system

114
00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.720
and price it at a set amount of, let's say, $3,000.

115
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:07.080
It was included there with the automated email sequences

116
00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.720
that respond to leads in other five minutes, for example.

117
00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:11.040
And so that's the key part of this.

118
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.640
When you package something, you always need to give a name to it,

119
00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.840
and it needs to be templated at some way.

120
00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:20.080
In fact, to give an even clearer example, if you just hop over to our classroom

121
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.920
and you just read through some of the names of the R lessons here,

122
00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:27.000
you may actually notice a pattern that almost all of them have this productized

123
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.720
package mindset built in.

124
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.200
So you have, for example, the creative cloner AI agent,

125
00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.910
you have the cinematic adverse agent, and each of these are a package by itself

126
00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:35.120
.

127
00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:38.530
And if you really think about it, our community itself is an example of a

128
00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:39.760
productized offer, right?

129
00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:43.600
So this is me and my team serving thousands of people automatically.

130
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.360
We, of course, still do custom solutions, but now a large part of our business

131
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.250
is building out these agents so that more people can use them at a set amount

132
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.000
of price.

133
00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:54.120
And so that's what I encourage you to think about.

134
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.000
On your specific niche, on your specific service,

135
00:05:57.000 --> 00:06:00.800
what are some of the common patterns that your industry has been asking for?

136
00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:04.360
And maybe it's actually time to start packaging that and giving it a name

137
00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.550
so that the next time that you offer it, it's already packaged, it's already

138
00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:07.720
set,

139
00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.920
and it has clear instructions on how you can give it to the next client.

140
00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.960
And just to hone in on this point, the three key reasons why productizing works

141
00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:18.840
is that they are easier to sell, they're easier to price, and they're also

142
00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.040
easier to deliver.

143
00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.000
They're easier to sell because prospects can immediately understand

144
00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:24.960
what they're getting without doing any guesses.

145
00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.880
They're easier to price because you know exactly what you're delivering.

146
00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:31.600
Pricing is straightforward and can be consistent no matter how you deliver it.

147
00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:34.960
And also it's obviously easier to deliver because you have done it before,

148
00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.800
you have a process and you get faster each time.

149
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.560
And finally, this actually positions you as a specialist

150
00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:44.600
because now you're starting to be less of a generalist doing whatever it is

151
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:47.280
that come your way, but now you're becoming the person

152
00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.280
for this specific thing in your immediate locality.

153
00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.940
And so to give you a few more thoughts on how you can actually come up with a

154
00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:54.640
product I offer,

155
00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:56.280
there's a few questions that you can ask.

